* '''Mod Ed:''' Some of the stuff there is not Mahjarrat related at all. It's just a nice little bit of flavour to add some life to the area and potentially be explored one day but doesn't have to be. The answer is a mixture of yes, maybe and no, depending on which bits you're looking at.

==General Lore Q&A==
===Question #15===
{{Cquote2|When creating the lore for a quest, what is the process like between you and other developers?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BKhhfypKNQY&t=2505s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ed:''' It depends from quest to quest, to be honest. The actual process of creating the lore itself varies from quest to quest, depending on a lot of things. Like we just talked about with Secrets of the North, we look to RuneScape and see what they've done to make sure we're being respectful of that, but also adding our own elements.
* '''Mod Ed:''' With something like adding a new Kourend quest, it has no connection to Runescape whatsoever, so we're not looking at Runescape at all, particularly.
* '''Mod Ed:''' The actual process of creating lore itself can vary quite a bit when working with other developers; it also depends on what type of project it is. Some quests are a standalone piece of content, where we'll just release a quest and nothing else, for example, Garden of Death was like that.
* '''Mod Ed:''' If it’s just the quest, in most cases it’s normally just myself working on them, but when we’re working on things like Secrets of the North and Desert Treasure 2 where there's post-quest content like bosses and new areas, then there's normally quite a few of us working on it.
* '''Mod Ed:''' It's normally me taking a lead on the lore, and when working with other developers, it is often a fun back and forth of - you might have somebody who's thinking more about the gameplay elements or like the mechanics of the boss fight. I’ll be there going “Well, that doesn't quite fit the lore”, and they’ll respond with “But it's really cool!” and I go “Alright, let me see if I can make this work!”
* '''Mod Ed:''' The ultimate example of that was Theatre of Blood, though it wasn’t actually a quest. For the Theatre of Blood, we decided quite early on in development that we want to do it in Morytania, and at the time, we hadn't really done much content in Morytania.
* '''Mod Ed:''' Since it was in Morytania, obviously it’s going to be vampyre-themed. Soon after that, the suggestion came up of “Let's lean in heavily on the vampyre society aspect of the lore, and with the way the lords and ladies act, let’s make it a theatre; a place of entertainment!”
* '''Mod Ed:''' Once those things have been established, it fell to me to fill in all the details like “Okay, we want to do a vampyre theatre in Morytania, and what is the actual lore for that?”. I came up with lots of characters, ideas and backstories for bosses, but when we were midway through development; we were sort of under the impression all of this would be finalised, Mod Ghost comes along and he goes “I've got an idea to give the final boss Verzik more ‘umph’.”
* '''Mod Ed:''' At the time, Mod Kieren was working on it and he wanted to do an additional phase at the end. We had designed her to be a two-phased boss, and it was not enough - she needed a third phase.
* '''Mod Ed:''' Mod Ghost comes along and goes “I've got this idea for a third phase. We've got these spiders in other parts of the raid, she turns into one!”. I'm just sitting there like; “A vampyre turns into a spider? That's not what we wrote!”. So I had to go away mid-development, and I basically rewrote Verzik’s story to the one that is presented in the Night at the Theatre quest. All of that only came about because someone came along and went “Spider, that'd be cool”! 
* '''Mod Ed:''' I hope it doesn't sound like I'm moaning about that. At the time, it was probably very much “Oh God, what am I going to do”, but that is one of the really good parts about game development. Sometimes it really forces you to go back to the drawing board and come up with ideas that are even cooler than before. 
* '''Mod Ed:''' When someone like Mod Ghost goes on and goes “I want to turn it into a spider”, it was a really good opportunity to go back and go “Okay, how can we make this work?” and the end result was something that was probably far, far superior to the original story, which was just that she's just a boring old vampyre. He gave us the opportunity to be really fun and creative.
* '''Mod Ed:''' The fun part is, it was all written back for the Theatre of Blood, but we didn't properly explore it until three years later with Night at the Theatre. It just sat there. We always like to establish the backstory of characters even if it's not going to be used in the piece of content we're making them for because chances are there'll be some update in the future that requires us to explore those characters more deeply and it ensures that things always stay really nice and consistent.
* '''Mod Ed:''' It's always really fun going back to something that was made three years earlier and finally revealing it to people.
* '''Mod Sarnie:''' I suppose that vampires and spiders have got a lot more in common, considering the first game update I was here for - which was in 2020 I believe - I found out spiders spawned the vampires in Morytania. Maybe they do have more in common than we thought!
* '''Mod Ed:''' There are some bugs that are bad bugs. That was a good one.

===Question #16===
{{Cquote2|Is the world of OSRS actually going to step forward and progress? Storylines are "progressing" but they don't seem to matter in the grand scheme of things other than end-game rewards. Quests seem to have no implications on the world as if they're all in their own self-contained bubble.}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BKhhfypKNQY&t=2911s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ed:''' I'm insulted! I guess it depends on what you mean and what your definition of progression is. I’m probably a little bit biased, but I do feel that there is a meaningful progression in our storylines and there is stuff that is impacting the world. Some of that is very small; changes to certain characters, but some of that is significantly bigger.
* '''Mod Ed:''' You complete the Song of the Elves and a whole new city has appeared. Another city that was once full of mourners - they've now all gone and they’re now starting to rebuild. That feels to me that it is impacting the game world in a very meaningful way. 
* '''Mod Ed:''' If that is the definition of progression, I - politely - disagree with the person asking this question! I do think they are having a meaningful impact on the wider world in that sense.
* '''Mod Ed:''' If by progression you’re referring to the game being set in the same year and thinking about how RuneScape has progressed to a new age when the Gods came back - no, that’s not what we want to do.
* '''Mod Ed:''' Part of Old School is that it is “old school”. We add new content to Old School weekly, but the way we add it is if done well, the idea is that the content feels like it could have been present in old school RuneScape as it was back then. It’s new, but it feels old school.
* '''Mod Ed:''' We take the same attitude to the narrative. With Old School RuneScape, it’s so important that the world always feels familiar; always feels like home. That is the foundation that Old School was built on. Progressing the storyline in a way that would move to a new age; that Gods would come back and Lumbridge blows up, I’m not saying those are bad things to do. It’s great, but RuneScape already did them.
* '''Mod Ed:''' The fact that RuneScape did them is really beneficial for both games because it means if you want that kind of story, you've got a RuneScape story, and if you want more old school, you've got the Old School story.
* '''Mod Ed:''' We don't need to do it as well; we can just stay in the fifth age and keep telling stories about a world where the Gods aren't present and focus on what the followers of the Gods do when they don't have a God to tell them what to do. It's all about their own interpretations, rather than the direction Runescape went in. So if that's the definition of progress, then no, that's not something we will ever do in Old School.

===Question #17===
{{Cquote2|I'd like to ask about Quest Backporting which was surveyed with high success: How is it planned to incorporate the quests into OSRS, having noted that the OSRS devs are trying to incorporate their own spin on the story?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BKhhfypKNQY&t=3126s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ed:''' We talked about this a bit earlier, and I said we’d hopefully talk about more at the end, we've managed to fit it in. So to reiterate, first of all, the survey showed really positive results. People were very interested in the idea of backporting quests with some caveats that we’re already on board with. 
* '''Mod Ed:''' The general consensus was that people didn't want to backport quests from after 2011, because that's when the storyline started to move towards the Gods returning, which feeds into the previous question. That's not the direction we want to take Old School in, so any backports would be limited to quests released prior to 2011. We would only backport the “good ones”, this was basically what the survey was saying, they were saying “don’t backport bad ones!”. No, we won’t backport bad quests! 
* '''Mod Ed:''' The quests that we asked about in that survey were specifically chosen based on that; what we felt were good quests and felt Old School. We didn't want quests that progress a story in a way that moved towards like Gods returning, and we don’t want them to interfere or conflict with our own narrative plans.
* '''Mod Ed:''' So that's why we asked about While Guthix Sleeps. We feel we can do While Guthix Sleeps with a few minor changes, and it would fit really nicely with our own plans for Mahjarrat. We didn't ask about Temple at Senntisten, we didn't ask about the Tale of Muspah and we didn't ask about the Ritual of Mahjarrat. None of those three would fit with what we are doing ourselves, but as with Secrets of the North, some small elements of those quests may still appear in other ways. 
* '''Mod Ed:''' When it comes to backporting, we'd like to do it in a way that compliments and adds to the quest that we're currently working on, rather than just “let's backport all the quests and not ever make any new Old School quests”. That's the aim of it. That was the feedback when we asked about and it was very early days when we got that feedback.
* '''Mod Ed:''' It was last year when we sent that survey around, and we did mention in the newspost at the time that that was basically Day One. It's a bit different to anything we've done before where normally we’d wait until later in the process, but there's no point even starting to explore this if people aren't interested, so we asked those questions very early on. 
* '''Mod Ed:''' It fully owned the expectation that it would not be explored in 2022, but we're not in 2022 anymore! I'm hoping we can start talking about this again a bit more soon and hopefully have some more concrete stuff to share.
* '''Mod Ed:''' Nothing today - we are in 2023, but it's still January, so we'll take a little time. It's something that we've had a few conversations about internally about what the next steps are now that we are into 2023 and planning some of that long-term stuff. Hopefully, we'll be able to share more soon.

